\section{Three identities, three reuse decisions}

A stable entity ID names a declaration across edits; an input key names a computation under specific dependencies; an installed version ID names a live code-and-metadata instance. Confusing them yields stale artifacts. In \emph{Editing Composed Languages}, Lukas Diekmann develops incremental parsing and abstract syntax trees to preserve and reuse parts of program structure while editing. Reusing an AST node is not proof that its resolved symbols are unchanged. Steven S. Muchnick's \emph{Advanced Compiler Design and Implementation} discusses preserving intermediate code for recompilation and integrating procedures across compilation units. Inlining illustrates why signatures and syntax identities alone do not suffice: the calling artifact now depends on a particular callee body or a summary that justifies the emitted code.

Dossier Chapters~07 and~22 sketch memoized compiler queries that record consumed dependencies. The blueprint uses immutable program revisions for consistent query reads. A sound realization checks a cached result against the actual input and query fingerprints observed last time. Updating a dependency may cause re-execution without changing the query's output; if that output is the only fact downstream consumed, stop invalidation there. Track diagnostic or source-location outputs separately from semantic IR where the engine allows it. A comment edit might preserve instructions but move a breakpoint or exception location.

\section{Edit a leaf, keep the independent work}

Suppose \texttt{twice(x)} originally returns \texttt{x+x} with wrapping arithmetic. \texttt{g(x)} inlines it; \texttt{h(x)} calls it indirectly via a dispatch cell, with no effect or result assumptions beyond the unchanged signature and ABI. Now edit \texttt{twice} to \texttt{x+x+1}. The prototype records these read sets (simplified to omit stable built-in dependencies):

\begin{verbatim}
emit(g): [body(g), body(twice), target, vm_abi]
emit(h): [body(h), signature(twice), target, vm_abi]
emit(twice): [body(twice), target, vm_abi]

get(q, revision):
  old = lookup(q)
  if old and all(current_fp(k, revision) == fp
                 for (k, fp) in old.reads):
    return old.result
  return run_recording_reads(q, revision)
\end{verbatim}

For argument 3 the old results were 6. The new \texttt{g} must be rebuilt; \texttt{h} may reuse its code and get 7 via the cell for the new revision. Define the edit policy to pin the current module revision per top-level invocation so an old live frame cannot silently call a new semantic revision. Install the new set of cells atomically as one revision; old invocations retain their view. A worker returning old \texttt{g} after publication of the edit cannot use its stable entity ID as permission to install. Test this race, and test a signature or VM ABI change, which invalidates \texttt{h} as well. Ensure lookup failure or a negative name-resolution result also records the scope that made the result true.

\section{Reusable bytes are not an installed activation}

Mehta and colleagues' \emph{Reusing Just-in-Time Compiled Code} indexes compiled R function versions by their specialization context and curates a persistent repository. Contextual dispatch selects a candidate without making the observed context a universal property of the function. Park and colleagues' \emph{Reusing the Optimized Code for JavaScript Ahead-of-Time Compilation} addresses a second problem: optimized machine code may contain process-specific addresses, so reuse includes relocation and handling profile-based specialization. Neither mechanism can be reduced to comparing source checksums. Include target features, backend and stencil version, VM ABI, relocation schema, and relevant semantic dependencies in reuse criteria. A profile may rank several valid artifacts; if the pipeline consumed profile data to produce an exact artifact, include the appropriate profile fingerprint in the reproducibility key. Guard and dependency validity still must be checked at installation and where required during execution.

Dossier Chapter~44 proposes hermetic compilation capsules; Chapter~21 discusses reachability-based retirement. A plausible capsule stores a reproducibility manifest, relocatable code, recovery metadata and the verdicts of the checks actually run. Hydration gives the artifact a new in-process version identity and fixes process-local relocations before publication. The capsule cannot promise exact output bytes if it omits inputs such as layout randomization, nor can a digest alone justify semantic reuse. Replay the edit sequence with the cache disabled, then enabled; compare results, exceptions and observations, and report memo hits, recompiled functions, hydration cost and bytes held by old frames separately (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-255}).
